\f1\fs32\b Before the face of God\b0\fs24\par
\f1\fs20 Torah: Leviticus 16:1\u8211?34 | Haftarah: Isaiah 57:14\u8211?58:14 | Gospel: Matthew 25:31\u8211?46\par
\f0\fs22\i Yom Kippur begins not with our experience but with God\u8217?s holiness: Aaron draws near in God\u8217?s way, receives atonement first for himself, and the guilt is carried away \u8212? \u8220?before the LORD you shall be clean.\u8221? Isaiah 58 unmasks a fast that stays mere form, and Matthew 25 tests the fruit: whoever truly lives from atonement cannot let the least become invisible. Hebrews 9\u8211?10 points to Yeshua, the High Priest who by His own blood appears \u8220?in the presence of God for us.\u8221?\i0\par
\par\f0\fs24 
\par\b\fs28 \u167?0 Observation round\b0\fs24\par


\par\b\fs24 0.1 Blind observation\b0\fs24\par


\par Leviticus 16 opens after the death of Nadab and Abihu (16:1): drawing near to God is not self-evident. Aaron may not enter at any moment, and must first make atonement for himself (16:6,11) before he can draw near for the people. Not only people but the sanctuary too is cleansed (16:16): sin touches the holy space. Two goats (16:8\u8211?10): one for the LORD, one for Azazel, led away bearing the confessed guilt (16:21\u8211?22) \u8212? sin is named and carried away. The whole is \u8220?once a year\u8221? (16:34), and the heart: \u8220?before the LORD you shall be clean\u8221? (16:30). Ending: affliction and sabbath rest (16:29\u8211?31). Isaiah 58 unmasks a fast that stays form and hides injustice; Matthew 25 separates sheep and goats on the treatment of the least.\par


\par\b\fs24 0.2 The four axes as an open question\b0\fs24\par


\par Does the weight fall on what God is and does (Source: holiness, atonement as God\u8217?s work), on the wholeness of inner and outer (Coherence: received atonement \u8596? treatment of the neighbour), on the response (Will: humbling, resting, receiving), or on the movement through time (Trajectory: the feasts, and fulfilment in Yeshua)?\par


\par\b\fs24 0.3 The centre of gravity read off\b0\fs24\par


\par \u-10179?\u-8222? Load-bearing are \b Source\b0  and \b Coherence\b0 : God is holy and gives the atonement (Aaron draws near in God\u8217?s way; cleansing \u8220?before His face\u8221?), and that received atonement becomes visible in how we treat the least (Isa. 58 / Matt. 25). Centre of gravity: standing before the face of God \u8212? and whoever has been there cannot let the other become invisible.\par


\par\b\fs24 0.4 Framing test\b0\fs24\par


\par \u-10179?\u-8908? Guarded. The weekly reflex lands on \u8220?receiving\u8221?; here that is grounded (atonement is God\u8217?s work, 16:30), but text and constellation push on to Coherence (inner\u8596?outer). Two guards: (1) the turn to the neighbour must not become works-righteousness \u8212? fruit shows the root, is not the root (Matt. 25 does not reverse Lev. 16); (2) the Hebrews layer must not flatten Leviticus 16 into one-to-one typology: the pattern points, it does not settle the account.\par



\par\b\fs28 \u167?1 The thread\b0\fs24\par


\par Atonement begins with God\u8217?s holiness, not with our experience; we receive access, we do not grasp it. Guilt is not only named but carried away \u8212? and whoever receives that God sees him and carries his guilt away begins to see the other and stops holding on to another\u8217?s guilt.\par



